Two non-obvious behaviors in the C had to be reproduced rather than "fixed":[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[1mThe classification functions return bitmasks, not 0/1.[22m glibc implements [38;5;10misalpha[0m and friends as macros that mask a table entry and hand back the raw bits, so [38;5;10mprintf("%d", isalpha('a'))[0m prints [38;5;10m1024[0m, not [38;5;10m1[0m. Each predicate has its own magic number — [38;5;10misalnum[0m→8, [38;5;10misupper[0m→256, [38;5;10mislower[0m→512, [38;5;10misdigit[0m→2048, [38;5;10misxdigit[0m→4096, [38;5;10misspace[0m→8192, [38;5;10misprint[0m→16384, [38;5;10misgraph[0m→32768, [38;5;10misblank[0m→1, [38;5;10miscntrl[0m→2, [38;5;10mispunct[0m→4. I verified these empirically against the compiled binary rather than assuming them.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[1m[38;5;10mchar c = getchar()[0m truncates.[22m [38;5;10mchar[0m is signed on x86-64 Linux, so input bytes 0x80–0xFF arrive at the ctype functions as −128..−1 after integer promotion, and EOF stays −1 (colliding with byte 0xFF). In the [38;5;10m"C"[0m locale glibc's tables cover −128..255 with all-zero classifications and identity case mapping in the negative region, so high bytes report "nothing at all" and echo back unchanged. The translation models the same −128-biased table layout.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
Output is assembled as raw bytes, not a [38;5;10mString[0m, because [38;5;10mprintf("%c")[0m emits NUL for input byte 0 and non-UTF-8 bytes for the high range — [38;5;10mprintln![0m would not round-trip those.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
Structure: [38;5;10msrc/ctype.rs[0m holds the const-built glibc tables and predicate wrappers; [38;5;10msrc/main.rs[0m mirrors the original's [38;5;10mdriver[0m/[38;5;10mmain[0m split. I did the work directly rather than delegating, so there are no sub-agent claims outstanding.[0m[0m
